          <h4 class="prose-h3">True native controls on Apple &amp; Windows</h4>
          <p class="prose-body">
            CMP paints shared UI onto Skia and hosts desktop in a JVM/Swing shell. Osprey Native renders real SwiftUI on macOS/iOS
            and real WinUI on Windows &mdash; or switches to Custom Canvas whenever you want identical pixels.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="prose-card cmp-card">
          <div class="cmp-icon" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polygon points="13 2 3 14 12 14 11 22 21 10 12 10 13 2"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h4 class="prose-h3">No JVM on desktop, no Kotlin lock-in</h4>
          <p class="prose-body">
            Every native target compiles AOT to machine code with a zero-serialization shared-memory ABI. Teams write in Default Osprey,
            ML, Python, or TypeScript flavor today, with Dart flavor coming soon.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="prose-card cmp-card">
          <div class="cmp-icon" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="5" r="2"/><path d="M5 9h14M12 9v5m-3 7 3-7 3 7"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h4 class="prose-h3">Structural accessibility for AI agents</h4>
          <p class="prose-body">
            UI machines make roles and live labels structural instead of optional modifiers, so screen readers, headless
            <code>@osprey/uitest</code> runs, and AI agents share one verified interaction surface.
          </p>
